{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git status --short; cat ANSWERS.md'","aggregated_output":"?? ANSWERS.md\n## Q1\n\nShipping is free for orders worth **€60.00 or more after discounts**, to a supported EU destination. Destinations outside the supported EU set are rejected before the free-shipping check. The default threshold and its meaning are defined in `parcels/settings.py:14-15`; the destination and inclusive threshold checks are in `parcels/quote.py:25-32`.\n\nChange the threshold without editing code by setting the environment variable `PARCELS_FREE_FROM`, for example `PARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00 python -m parcels 75.00 3.2 DE`. Settings are read once at import, so set the variable before starting the application (restart an already running process). See `parcels/settings.py:1-6` and `parcels/settings.py:15`.\n\n## Q2\n\nThe single-parcel limit is **31.5 kg** (`parcels/settings.py:17`). A heavier order is split into the fewest possible parcels: the count is the total weight divided by the limit, rounded up. Each parcel gets the same weight, rounded up to the nearest gram. For example, 70 kg becomes three parcels of 23.334 kg each. See `parcels/packing.py:11-22` and the example test in `tests/test_parcels.py:11-12`.\n\nFor paid shipping, the quote sums the shipping price of every parcel; an order qualifying for free shipping returns zero before packing is calculated. See `parcels/quote.py:31-33`.\n\n## Q3\n\nThe default carrier is **PostNL** (`postnl`). Choose another by setting `PARCELS_CARRIER` to `dhl` or `ups` before starting the application, for example `PARCELS_CARRIER=dhl python -m parcels 45.00 3.2 DE`. The value is lowercased, and an unsupported value raises `RuntimeError`. Settings are read once at import. See `parcels/settings.py:1` and `parcels/settings.py:8-12`.\n\nThe selected carrier determines the tariff used to price each parcel (`parcels/quote.py:17-22`; tariff values: `parcels/carriers.py:16-20`).\n\n## Q4\n\nFor an order placed on **Friday at 17:00**, the 15:00 cutoff moves the starting date to Saturday. The calculation then adds **one handling day plus the destination's transit days**, counting working days strictly after that starting date. See `parcels/delivery.py:25-30`, `parcels/delivery.py:14-22`, and `parcels/settings.py:18`.\n\nThe resulting delivery date depends on the destination (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`):\n\n| Destination | Handling + transit | Delivery after that Friday |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Netherlands (`NL`) | 1 + 1 = 2 working days | Tuesday |\n| Belgium, Germany, Luxembourg (`BE`, `DE`, `LU`) | 1 + 2 = 3 working days | Wednesday |\n| Other supported destinations | 1 + 4 = 5 working days | The following Friday |\n\n**Weekends are skipped; public holidays are not.** The working-day loop only checks whether a date is Monday through Friday, so a public holiday on a weekday still counts toward delivery. See `parcels/delivery.py:14-22`. The delivery calculation uses the destination, without consulting the configured carrier (`parcels/delivery.py:25-30`).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
